### 2. LocalAI vs Omni Route — They're Different, You Pick One (or Layer Them)

They're two different products with significant overlap:

| | LocalAI (mudler) | OmniRoute (diegosouzapw) |
|---|---|---|
| **What it is** | Local inference server (Go) | Multi-provider routing gateway (Next.js) |
| **OpenAI-compatible API** | Yes | Yes (`/v1/*`) |
| **Local model hosting** | Yes (core feature) | No (routes TO local runtimes like llama.cpp, Ollama) |
| **Cloud model routing** | Yes (cloud-proxy backend) | Yes (350 providers, 19 routing strategies) |
| **OpenAI↔Anthropic translation** | Yes (translate mode) | Yes (request/response translation) |
| **Quota-aware routing** | No | Yes (quota-share, P2C account selection) |
| **Modality bridge** | Limited | Yes (vision + audio + video) |

**Your instinct is right** — running both is redundant for routing. Two options:
- **Option A**: Use OmniRoute as your routing layer, point it at your GenieX/QAIRT local runtime via the `llama.cpp` or `openai-compatible-*` provider card. Skip LocalAI.
- **Option B**: Use LocalAI as your local inference server + OpenAI↔Anthropic translation, skip OmniRoute. Simpler, fewer moving parts on the phone.

For a phone-only setup with two NPU-pinned local models, **Option A (OmniRoute only)** makes more sense — it handles the modality routing and translation, and you point it at your local GenieX endpoint. LocalAI would be extra weight on the phone for no additional benefit since you're not hosting multiple local model formats.

